## Rigorous Experimental Control

To isolate the impact of the grouping boundary, the researchers utilized a "frozen manifest" methodology. They first collected a large dataset of trajectories using four production harnesses—Aider, OpenHands, Qwen Code, and SWE-agent—across a set of SWE-Gym tasks.

Every RL training run started from the same supervised fine-tuning (SFT) warm start of a Qwen3-8B model. Crucially, every training arm replayed the exact same tokens, records, and rewards. This means that if the Within-harness and Cross-harness models ended up with different behaviors, it could only be because of how the advantage $\hat{A}$A^ was calculated and distributed during the GRPO update step:

$$L_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau \in G} \left[ \min\left( \rho_t(\theta)\hat{A}(\tau), \text{clip}(\rho_t(\theta), 1-\epsilon, 1+\epsilon)\hat{A}(\tau) \right) - \beta D_{KL}(\pi_{\theta} \| \pi_{\text{ref}}) \right]$$L GRPO​(θ)=E τ∈G​[min(ρ t​(θ)A^(τ),clip(ρ t​(θ),1−ϵ,1+ϵ)A^(τ))−β D K L​(π θ​∥π ref​)]
## Portability vs. Configuration Adaptation

The central question was whether Cross-harness credit assignment leads to better "portability"—the ability of the agent to perform well in a completely new, unseen harness. To test this, the researchers evaluated all policies on a "held-out" harness called weak-ReAct, which was not used during training.

The results showed no significant difference in portability between the training rules. On the held-out harness, the solve rates for the Within, Cross, and even the basic SFT model were statistically indistinguishable. Even after increasing the evaluation budget to $k=8$k=8 attempts per task to gain more precision, the gap between Cross and Within remained a negligible +0.16 percentage points.

While RL did improve performance on the "source" harnesses seen during training, these gains did not transfer to the unseen environment. This indicates that the learning occurring during multi-harness RL is primarily "configuration adaptation"—the agent is learning to better navigate the specific interfaces and quirks of Aider or OpenHands, rather than developing a more robust, generalized understanding of how to fix software bugs.
